{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-64560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=y+4x+{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =y(0)+4(0)+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1+0+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}={y}^{′}+4+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{′}(0)+4+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =-4+4+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =1+x\\cdot (-4)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot 2+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot 1+⋯ \\\\  & =1-4x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64560" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-64560" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64560" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=1+4x+2{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1+4x+2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{3}⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1-4x+{x}^{2}+2{x}^{3}⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1-4x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1-4x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64560" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=1-4x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=y-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-2,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =y(0)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =-2-2(0) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}={y}^{′}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{′}(0)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-2(1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinaly, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\ y(x) & =-2+x\\cdot 2+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot 0+⋯ \\\\ y(x) & =-2+2x-{x}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-64558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=-2+2x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-2+2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-2+2x-{x}^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-2+x+{x}^{2}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-2+x-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=-2+2x-{x}^{2}+⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2y-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-2,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =2y(0)-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =2(-2)-6(1) \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}=2{y}^{′}+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =2{y}^{′}(0)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2(2)+0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinaly, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\ y(x) & =-2+x\\cdot 2+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot (-10)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot 4+⋯ \\\\ y(x) & =-2+2x-5{x}^{2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79943" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-79943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2+2x+2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}⋯"] ["math" "y=-2+2x+\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}⋯"] ["math" "y=-2+2x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}⋯"] ["math" "y=-2+2x-5{x}^{2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}⋯"] ["math" "y=-2+2x-5{x}^{2}+\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79943" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-2+2x-5{x}^{2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=3y+4{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-2,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =3y(0)+4{e}^{2(0)} \\\\  & =3(-2)+4(1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}=3{y}^{′}+8{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =3{y}^{′}(0)+8{e}^{2(0)} \\\\  & =3(0)+8 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinaly, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =-2+x\\cdot 0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot 8+⋯ \\\\  & =-2-{x}^{2}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64557" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-64557" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64557" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=-2+3{x}^{2}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-2+{x}^{2}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-2-3{x}^{2}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-2-{x}^{2}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-2-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64557" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=-2-{x}^{2}+\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=y+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =y(0)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-1+2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}={y}^{′}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{′}(0)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-0-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x\\cdot 0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot 0+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-64503" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64503" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=-1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-1-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-1-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64503" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=-1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=1$, in ascending powers of $(x-1)$ up to and including the term in $(x-1)^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=1$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(1)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(1).$\n\nWe substitute $y(1)=2$ and ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & ={y}^{2}(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-{y}^{′}(1) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0-1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=2y{y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}-{y}^{″}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(1)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(1) & =2y(1){y}^{′}(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{{y}^{2}(1)}{1}-{y}^{″}(1) \\\\  & =2(2)(1)(0)+\\frac{(2)^{2}}{1}-(-1) \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinaly, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯ \\\\  & =2+(x-1)\\cdot 1+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{6}\\cdot 5+… \\\\  & =2+(x-1)-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{6}(x-1)^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-79947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2+(x-1)+\\frac{3(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=2+(x-1)-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=2+(x-1)-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{6}(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=2+(x-1)-\\frac{3(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{7}{6}(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=2-(x-1)-\\frac{3(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{6}(x-1)^{3}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=2+(x-1)-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{6}(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=\\pi $, in ascending powers of $(x-\\pi )$ up to and including the term in $(x-\\pi )^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=\\pi $:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\pi ),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(\\pi )$ and ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi ).$\n\nWe substitute $y(\\pi )=1$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(\\pi ) & ={y}^{2}(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot (0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=2y{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(\\pi )$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi ) & =2y(\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+{y}^{2}(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =2(1)(-1)(0)+(1)^{2}(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(\\pi )+(x-\\pi ){y}^{′}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(\\pi )+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(\\pi )+⋯ \\\\  & =1+(x-\\pi )\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{2}}{2}\\cdot (0)+\\frac{(x-\\pi )^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-1)+… \\\\  & =1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-79945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=1+(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=1+(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=1-(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=-1$, in ascending powers of $(x+1)$ up to and including the term in $(x+1)^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}={x}^{2}y-{y}^{′},\\,y(-1)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=-1$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(-1)+(x+1){y}^{′}(-1)+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(-1)+\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(-1)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(-1)$ and ${y}^{′}(-1),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(-1)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(-1).$\n\nWe substitute $y(-1)=3$ and ${y}^{′}(-1)=0$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(-1) & =(-1)^{2}y(-1)-{y}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=2xy+{x}^{2}{y}^{′}-{y}^{″}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(-1)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(-1) & =2(-1)y(-1)+(-1)^{2}{y}^{′}(-1)-{y}^{″}(-1) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3)+((-1)^{2}\\cdot 0)-3 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(-1)+(x+1){y}^{′}(-1)+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(-1)+\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(-1)+⋯ \\\\  & =3+(x+1)\\cdot 0+\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}\\cdot 3+\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-9)+… \\\\  & =3+\\frac{3}{2}(x+1)^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}(x+1)^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64563" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-64563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=3+\\frac{3}{2}(x+1)^{2}+(x+1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=3+\\frac{3}{2}(x+1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}(x+1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=3+\\frac{3}{2}(x+1)^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}(x+1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=3-\\frac{1}{2}(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(x+1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=3-\\frac{1}{2}(x+1)^{2}-(x+1)^{3}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64563" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=3+\\frac{3}{2}(x+1)^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}(x+1)^{3}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=1$, in ascending powers of $(x-1)$ up to and including the term in $(x-1)^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=xy+{y}^{′},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=1$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(1)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(1).$\n\nWe substitute $y(1)=1$ and ${y}^{′}(1)=2$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =1\\cdot y(1)+{y}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=x{y}^{′}+y+{y}^{″}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(1)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(1) & =1\\cdot {y}^{′}(1)+y(1)+{y}^{″}(1) \\\\  & =2+1+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(1)+(x-1){y}^{′}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(1)+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(1)+⋯ \\\\  & =1+(x-1)\\cdot 2+\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}\\cdot 3+\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{6}\\cdot 6+… \\\\  & =1+2(x-1)+\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64504" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-64504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=1+2(x-1)+(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1+2(x-1)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+3(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1+2(x-1)+\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1+2(x-1)+\\frac{5}{6}(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1+2(x-1)^{2}-4(x-1)^{3}⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64504" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=1+2(x-1)+\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{3}+⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=2$, in ascending powers of $(x-2)$ up to and including the term in $(x-2)^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=y-{e}^{2-x},\\,y(2)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=2$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(2)+(x-2){y}^{′}(2)+\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(2)+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(2)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{″}(2)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(2).$\n\nWe substitute $y(2)=-1$ into the given equation to find ${y}^{″}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(2) & =y(2)-{e}^{2-2} \\\\  & =-1-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)={y}^{′}+{e}^{2-x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(1)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(2) & ={y}^{′}(2)+{e}^{2-2} \\\\  & =2+{e}^{2-2} \\\\  & =2+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(2)+(x-2){y}^{′}(2)+\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(2)+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(2)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+(x-2)\\cdot (2)+\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{6}\\cdot 3+… \\\\  & =-1+2(x-2)-(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{2}+…\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-79946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-1+2(x-2)+(x-2)^{2}-2(x-2)^{3}+…"] ["math" "y=-1+2(x-2)+2(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{2}+…"] ["math" "y=-1+2(x-2)-(x-2)^{2}+2(x-2)^{3}+…"] ["math" "y=-1+2(x-2)-(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{2}+…"] ["math" "y=-1+2(x-2)-2(x-2)^{2}-\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{2}+…"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-1+2(x-2)-(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{2}+…"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-y{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =(0)^{2}-y(0){e}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=2x-{y}^{′}{e}^{x}-y{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =2(0)-{y}^{′}(0){e}^{0}-y(0){e}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(1\\cdot 1)-(-1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(x) & =2-{y}^{″}{e}^{x}-2{y}^{′}{e}^{x}-y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =2-{y}^{″}(0){e}^{0}-2{y}^{′}(0){e}^{0}-y(0){e}^{0} \\\\  & =2-(0\\cdot 1)-(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1)-((-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =2-0-2+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x\\cdot (1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot 1+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-64565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-1+x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-1+x-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-1-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=-1-x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2(0)-y(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=2-{y}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =2-{y}^{′}(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+y(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =2-(1)(1)+(-1)(0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)=-{y}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-{y}^{″}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =-{y}^{″}(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2{y}^{′}(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+y(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =-(1)(1)+2(1)(0)+(-1)(1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-2)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-79951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-1+2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯"] ["math" "y=-1+2x+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{6}+⋯"] ["math" "y=-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=-1+x-{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-1+x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1+2x+xy,\\,y(0)=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=-1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =1+2(0)+(0)y(0) \\\\  & =1+0+0(-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=2+y+x{y}^{′}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =2+y(0)+(0){y}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =2+(-1)+(0)(1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)={y}^{′}+{y}^{′}+x{y}^{″}=2{y}^{′}+x{y}^{″}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =2{y}^{′}(0)+(0){y}^{″}(0) \\\\  & =2(1)+(0)(1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot (1)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot 2+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-79953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-1+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=-1+x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=-1+x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=-1+x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=-1+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-1+x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=yx+{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=0,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =y(0)(0)+{e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)={y}^{′}x+y+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & ={y}^{′}(0)(0)+y(0)+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)={y}^{″}x+{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}={y}^{″}x+2{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{″}(0)(0)+2{y}^{′}(0)+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =0+2(1)+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =0+x\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot 3+⋯ \\\\  & =x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-79950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=x+2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y=x+{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-79950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in ${x}^{3},$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{′}(0),$  ${y}^{″}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=1,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+y(0){e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{″}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=1+{y}^{′}{e}^{x}+y{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{″}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =1+{y}^{′}(0){e}^{0}+y(0){e}^{0} \\\\  & =1+1+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate ${y}^{″}(x)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}(x)={y}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{y}^{′}{e}^{x}+y{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{″}(0){e}^{0}+2{y}^{′}(0){e}^{0}+y(0){e}^{0} \\\\  & =3+2+1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =1+x\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 3+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot 6+⋯ \\\\  & =1+x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd5e9ac3a3cacc44141509cd7cab732b86463ca208d4ae40d490f9ac73e6e7e7"} {"question" "q-64509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y(x)=1+2x+3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1+x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1+x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1+x-{x}^{3}+⋯"] ["math" "y(x)=1-x+2{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3}+⋯"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "q-64509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y(x)=1+x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/state.json" "file_sha256" "b37f91d9d121905db2788f9347c6abd4e8c77b2bf077548baaa649140d7b7a8c"} {"question" "e-6667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0,$ in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in $x^3,$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny''=y+e^{-4x}, \\qquad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/example-6667.json" "file_sha256" "e585c6eba09caafd19bb96251dc0ee3da49605d58a24dadafa98375d438f7787"} {"question" "e-6667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny(x) = y(0) + xy'(0) + \\dfrac{x^2}{2!}y''(0) + \\dfrac{x^3}{3!}y^{(3)}(0)+\\cdots\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and $y'(0),$ so we need to calculate $y''(0)$ and $y^{(3)}(0).$\n\n- We know that $y(0)=0,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find $y''(0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =y(0)+{e}^{-4(0)} \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, to find an expression for $y^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\ny^{(3)}=y'-4e^{-4x}\n$$\n\nWe can now find  $y^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & ={y}^{′}(0)-4{e}^{-4(0)} \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =0+x\\cdot 2+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot 1+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot (-2)+⋯ \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/example-6667.json" "file_sha256" "e585c6eba09caafd19bb96251dc0ee3da49605d58a24dadafa98375d438f7787"} {"question" "e-6668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=2$, in ascending powers of $(x-2)$ up to and including the term in $(x-2)^3,$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny''=y-2xy', \\qquad y(2)=0, \\quad y'(2)=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/example-6668.json" "file_sha256" "4a0aece2b40fc184cb54950af2c1d1769876706cedf01f99a643b9284b56b2b5"} {"question" "e-6668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny(x) = y(2) + (x-2)y'(2) + \\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{2!}y''(2) + \\dfrac{(x-2)^3}{3!}y^{(3)}(2)+\\cdots\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(2)$ and $y'(2),$ so we need to calculate $y''(2)$ and $y^{(3)}(2).$\n\n- We substitute $y(2)=0$ and $y'(2)=1$ into the given equation to find $y''(2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(2) & =y(2)-2(2){y}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =0-4 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, to find an expression for $y^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\ny^{(3)}(x)=y' -2y' - 2xy'' = -y'- 2xy''\n$$\n\nWe can now find  $y^{(3)}(2)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(2) & =-{y}^{′}(2)-2(2){y}^{″}(2) \\\\  & =-1+16 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(2)+(x-2){y}^{′}(2)+\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(2)+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(2)+⋯ \\\\  & =0+(x-2)\\cdot 1+\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}\\cdot (-4)+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{6}\\cdot 15+… \\\\  & =(x-2)-2(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{5(x-2)^{3}}{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/example-6668.json" "file_sha256" "4a0aece2b40fc184cb54950af2c1d1769876706cedf01f99a643b9284b56b2b5"} {"question" "e-6669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0,$ in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in $x^3,$  of the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'=2x-y \\cos x, \\qquad y(0)=4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/example-6669.json" "file_sha256" "843f9cb32dbc1328e41a940ec095176cafe0b13560ea7bb6d86b8b07a1e85bad"} {"question" "e-6669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny(x) = y(0) + xy'(0) + \\dfrac{x^2}{2!}y''(0) + \\dfrac{x^3}{3!}y^{(3)}(0)+\\cdots\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0),$ so we need to calculate $y'(0),$  $y''(0),$ and $y^{(3)}(0).$\n\n- We know that $y(0)=4,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find $y'(0)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2(0)-y(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0-4(1) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, to find an expression for $y''(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\ny''(x)=2-y'\\cos x + y \\sin x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  $y''(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(0) & =2-{y}^{′}(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+y(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =2-(-4)(1)+0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find an expression for $y^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate $y''(x)$ with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\ny^{(3)}(x)=-y''\\cos x + 2y'\\sin x + y \\cos x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  $y^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =-{y}^{″}(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+{y}^{′}(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+y(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =-(6)(1)+(-4)(0)+(4)(1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{′}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{″}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+⋯ \\\\  & =4+x\\cdot (-4)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot 6+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}\\cdot (-2)+⋯ \\\\  & =4-4x+3{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069991/example-6669.json" "file_sha256" "843f9cb32dbc1328e41a940ec095176cafe0b13560ea7bb6d86b8b07a1e85bad"}] "usage" {"e-6668" [] "q-64503" [] "q-64504" [] "e-6669" [] "e-6667" [] "q-64509" []} "basis" 2426 "retractions" [[17592186148105 :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x = 0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in $x^{3}$, of the initial value problem\n\n$y^{″} = y + 2\\cos (x), y(0) =-1, y^{′}(0) = 0$."] [17592186148106 :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x = 1$, in ascending powers of $(x - 1)$ up to and including the term in $(x - 1)^{3}$, of the initial value problem\n\n$y^{″} = xy + y^{′}, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) = 2$."] [17592186148107 :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x = 0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in $x^{3}$, of the initial value problem\n\n$y^{′} = x + ye^{x}, y(0) = 1$."] [17592186147307 :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in $x^3$, of the initial value problem\n$y''=y+e^{-4x}, \\qquad y(0)=0, \\quad y'(0)=2$."] [17592186147307 :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x) = y(0) + xy'(0) + \\dfrac{x^2}{2!}y''(0) + \\dfrac{x^3}{3!}y^{(3)}(0)+\\cdots\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and $y'(0)$, so we need to calculate $y''(0)$ and $y^{(3)}(0)$.\n\n- We know that $y(0)=0$, so we substitute this into the given equation to find $y''(0)$:\n$y^{″}(0)|= y(0) + e^{-4(0)}; = 0 + 1; = 1$\n- Next, to find an expression for $y^{(3)}(x)$, we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x$:\n$y^{(3)}=y'-4e^{-4x}$\nWe can now find $y^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n$y^{(3)}(0)|= y^{′}(0) - 4e^{-4(0)}; = 2 - 4; =-2$\n\nFinally, we substitute the obtained values in the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y(0) + xy^{′}(0) + \\frac{x^{2}}{2!}y^{″}(0) + \\frac{x^{3}}{3!}y^{(3)}(0) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 0 + x \\cdot 2 + \\frac{x^{2}}{2!} \\cdot 1 + \\frac{x^{3}}{3!} \\cdot (-2) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 2x + \\frac{x^{2}}{2} - \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186147308 :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=2$, in ascending powers of $(x-2)$ up to and including the term in $(x-2)^3$, of the initial value problem\n$y''=y-2xy', \\qquad y(2)=0, \\quad y'(2)=1$."] [17592186147308 :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=2$:\n\n$$\ny(x) = y(2) + (x-2)y'(2) + \\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{2!}y''(2) + \\dfrac{(x-2)^3}{3!}y^{(3)}(2)+\\cdots\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(2)$ and $y'(2)$, so we need to calculate $y''(2)$ and $y^{(3)}(2)$.\n\n- We substitute $y(2)=0$ and $y'(2)=1$ into the given equation to find $y''(2)$:\n$y^{″}(2)|= y(2) - 2(2)y^{′}(2); = 0 - 4; =-4$\n- Next, to find an expression for $y^{(3)}(x)$, we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x$:\n$y^{(3)}(x)=-y' -2y' - 2xy'' = -3y'- 2xy''$\nWe can now find $y^{(3)}(2)$ by substituting in the known values:\n$y^{(3)}(2)|=-3y^{′}(2) - 2(2)y^{″}(2); =-3 + 16; = 13$\n\nFinally, we substitute the obtained values in the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y(2) + (x - 2)y^{′}(2) + ((x - 2)^{2})/(2!)y^{″}(2) + ((x - 2)^{3})/(3!)y^{(3)}(2) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 0 + (x - 2) \\cdot 1 + ((x - 2)^{2})/(2) \\cdot (-4) + ((x - 2)^{3})/(6) \\cdot 13 + … \\\\\n&= (x - 2) - 2(x - 2)^{2} + (13(x - 2)^{3})/(6) + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186147309 :question/problem "Use the Taylor series method to find a series solution about $x=0$, in ascending powers of $x$ up to and including the term in $x^3$, of the initial value problem\n$y'=2x-y \\cos x, \\qquad y(0)=4$."] [17592186147309 :question/worked-solution "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x) = y(0) + xy'(0) + \\dfrac{x^2}{2!}y''(0) + \\dfrac{x^3}{3!}y^{(3)}(0)+\\cdots\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$, so we need to calculate $y'(0)$, $y''(0)$, and $y^{(3)}(0)$.\n\n- We know that $y(0)=4$, so we substitute this into the given equation to find $y'(0)$:\n$y^{′}(0)|= 2(0) - y(0)\\cos 0; = 0 - 4(1); =-4$\n- Next, to find an expression for $y''(x)$, we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x$:\n$y''(x)=2-y'\\cos x + y \\sin x$\nWe can now find $y''(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n$y^{″}(0)|= 2 - y^{′}(0)\\cos 0 + y(0)\\sin 0; = 2 - (-4)(1) + 0; = 6$\n- To find an expression for $y^{(3)}(x)$, we differentiate $y''(x)$ with respect to $x$:\n$y^{(3)}(x)=-y''\\cos x + 2y'\\sin x + y \\cos x$\nWe can now find $y^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n$y^{(3)}(0)|=-y^{″}(0)\\cos 0 + y^{′}(0)\\sin 0 + y(0)\\cos 0; = - (6)(1) + (-4)(0) + (4)(1); =-2$\n\nFinally, we substitute the obtained values in the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= y(0) + xy^{′}(0) + \\frac{x^{2}}{2!}y^{″}(0) + \\frac{x^{3}}{3!}y^{(3)}(0) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 4 + x \\cdot (-4) + \\frac{x^{2}}{2} \\cdot 6 + \\frac{x^{3}}{6} \\cdot (-2) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 4 - 4x + 3x^{2} - \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186146642 17592186146643 17592186146644 17592186146645 17592186146646 17592186146648 17592186146649 17592186146650 17592186146651 17592186146652 17592186146654 17592186146655 17592186146656 17592186146657 17592186146658] "immutable_fields" [[17592186146641 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146641 :answer-field/id] [17592186146641 :answer-field/key] [17592186146641 :answer-field/type] [17592186146647 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146647 :answer-field/id] [17592186146647 :answer-field/key] [17592186146647 :answer-field/type] [17592186146653 :answer-field/correct] [17592186146653 :answer-field/id] [17592186146653 :answer-field/key] [17592186146653 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
